  <title>4.13.5: Graph of a Function and the Domain and Range Connection</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01088</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.13.5: Graph of a Function and the Domain and Range Connection</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fe1563f7-05db-4bcb-8c04-6d33699ce7cc</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>The function \(W\) gives the temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, of a pot of water on a stove \(t\) minutes after the stove is turned on. After 30 minutes, the pot is taken off the stove.</para>
<para>The graph of the function is shown.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="graph of temperature of water over time"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4c9e0f12217b960734c19d8111c00021400c1d4d"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Is 250 in the range of function \(W\)? Explain how you know. </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> No, 250 is not in the range. The highest temperature is 212 degrees Fahrenheit. </para>
  <item> Describe the range of the function. </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> The range is all values between 75 and 212, the lowest and the highest temperature, \(75\leq y\leq212.\) </para>
  <item> Does \(W(t)=0\) have a solution? Explain how you know. </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> No, a solution to this equation would be the time when the temperature is 0 degrees Fahrenheit, which is never the case. </para>
</list>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>If students are still struggling with domain and range, examine errors in the cool-down and make sure to discuss domain and range during each of the activities in Lesson 13.</para></section>
</content>
